From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 10:22:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] kk: MESA_KK_EXPERIMENTAL=private_memory, a private-storage
 memory type

With the flag, KosmicKrisp lists a second memory type, DEVICE_LOCAL
only, before its host-visible one. Its memory is a private-storage
placement heap (no CPU mapping, no buffer over the whole heap); every
other allocation stays a shared-storage heap as now.

Today every VkDeviceMemory is a shared-storage heap with a buffer over
all of it, so every render target DXVK and vkd3d-proton create is a
shared texture in a heap a buffer aliases (the step-0 capture: heap
resource options 0x100, texture storage mode 0), where DXMT creates its
render targets as private textures of their own. Metal compresses render
targets losslessly only under conditions this layout may not meet, and
the usage change alone moved nothing.

- DXVK, vkd3d-proton and the WSI take the lowest memory type that fits
  the flags they ask for, so what they ask for as DEVICE_LOCAL without
  HOST_VISIBLE lands in the new type; what they map they ask for as
  HOST_VISIBLE and still get the host-visible type.
- Images that cannot be private (linear: buffer-backed textures; host
  image copy; sparse; external) report only the host-visible type, as do
  host-pointer imports.
- An image that can be private reports the larger size and alignment of
  its texture in a shared and in a private heap, so it fits either.
- Buffers keep every memory type (vkd3d-proton clears image memory with
  a buffer bound to it) and take the larger buffer alignment of a shared
  and a private heap.
- A buffer placed in a heap takes the heap's resource options (Metal
  requires the heap's storage mode).

Without the flag the memory types and every allocation are as before.

Class: ios-port
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-08-vulkan-perf-private-memory.md
Offered-upstream: no (cf. mesa!44221, which adds the type but keeps its
 memory shared)
